          en: 'The technical process of programming begins long before the code editor. It starts with problem decomposition: dividing a complex task into manageable sub-tasks. A user registration system, for example, is not a single task. it\'s a set of operations: input data validation, uniqueness verification, password hashing, database persistence, email confirmation, error handling at each step. Each of these operations is a smaller problem that can be solved independently.\n\nAfter decomposition comes abstraction: identifying what is essential versus what is detail. A registration form and a profile update form share the same validation logic. the programmer who recognizes this similarity creates a reusable abstraction instead of duplicating code. Then comes pattern recognition: situations that repeat with variations. Iterating over a product list and iterating over a user list are the same pattern with different data. Finally, algorithmic thinking: defining the exact sequence of steps, considering alternative conditions, predicting failure scenarios, and ensuring every possible path is covered. This process is language-independent. It works in JavaScript, Python, Go, or any other technology.',
