          en: 'Understanding and memorization are fundamentally different cognitive processes. Memorizing is storing literal information for later retrieval. Understanding is building a mental model that allows applying knowledge in new contexts. When a programmer understands that a loop is a repetition structure that executes a block of code while a condition is true, they can use any loop variation in any language. When they only memorized the syntax "for (let i = 0; i < n; i++)", they\'re stuck to that specific form.\n\nThis distinction exists because programming deals with problems that never repeat identically. Each project has unique context, each bug has specific cause, each requirement has its own nuances. Memorization prepares for repetition. Understanding prepares for variation. The practical impact is measurable: the professional who understands concepts learns new technologies in a fraction of the time because they recognize familiar patterns in new contexts. The professional who depends on memorization needs to restart the memorizing process with every tool change.',
