          en: 'In daily work, systems thinking translates into questions the developer asks before implementing any change. "How does this change affect other components?" "What happens if this external service becomes unavailable?" "Will this query work with a hundred thousand records?" "How will we revert if this change causes problems?" These questions seem simple but prevent serious incidents.\n\nIn planning meetings, the professional with systemic vision identifies risks others don\'t see. They notice that a seemingly simple feature touches five different components and needs coordination. In incident post-mortems, they trace root cause through multiple layers instead of stopping at the first symptom. In building personal projects, they build end-to-end. from interface to deploy. because they understand that a functional system is more than code that compiles. This capability is exactly what companies seek when looking for professionals "with product vision" or "impact-oriented."',
